      "writtenOn": "2018-09-04T08:46:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is really great and looking almost complete. Because what\u0027s scary in this I think we may want to be more exhaustive on all the edges that should and should not trigger a callback, though.\n\nThe moving parts are :\n- The rules (NONE, REJECT_METERED, REJECT_ALL)\n- The meteredness\n- Data saver\n\nI think we want to check each edge of this – that is, try all possible combinations of change.\nnone false false → none false true\nnone false false → none true false\nnone false false → metered false false\nnone false false → all false false\nn f t → n f f\nn f t → n t t\nn f t → m f t\nn f t → a f t\nn t f → n t t, n f f, m t f, a t f\nn t t → n t f, n f t, m t t, a t t\nm f f → m f t, m t f, n f f, a f f\n...\n\nWhat that gives us is a helper function that takes the three initial inputs and the three final inputs, and a bool that tells whether the callback should be called.\n\nThen, another function that lists all pairs of triplets with the correct behavior. Maybe this one can be generated if the logic is simple enough (but I doubt it is), or maybe part of it could be.\n\nFinally, we should also test all possible combinations at network connection time to make sure the initial blocked status callback always has the right value.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4567,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 4567,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "9626da74d1bd000f58d4ec4ee8e96be34814cefd",
